// TASK 3: Trigger Design
$task_contents[3] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>What Starts a Playbook</h2>

    <p>Every playbook needs a starting point. That starting point is called a trigger, and choosing the right trigger type is one of the first design decisions in building any automated workflow. Pick the wrong one and the playbook either never runs or runs at the wrong time.</p>

    <p>There are four trigger types used across SOAR platforms, and each matches a different way an event can originate.</p>

    <h3>The Four Trigger Types</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Trigger Type</th>
                <th>Definition</th>
                <th>Example</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Alert-driven</strong></td>
                <td>Fires when a detection tool such as a SIEM generates an alert.</td>
                <td>A brute force login alert starts an account lockdown playbook.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Scheduled</strong></td>
                <td>Runs on a fixed time interval regardless of any alert.</td>
                <td>A threat intel feed sync that pulls new indicators every hour.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Event-driven</strong></td>
                <td>Fires when an external system generates a specific event, such as a webhook.</td>
                <td>A new ticket tagged phishing-reported triggers a triage playbook.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Manual</strong></td>
                <td>Started directly by an analyst.</td>
                <td>An analyst runs an account lockdown workflow during an active incident.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
