// TASK 5: Response Tool Integration
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Taking Action</h2>

    <p>Once a playbook has enough context to act, it needs a way to actually do something about the threat. Response integrations connect the SOAR platform to the tools that carry out containment and remediation actions.</p>

    <p>These integrations reach into endpoints, network infrastructure, and identity systems, and the actions they perform vary in how risky they are to run without human review.</p>

    <h3>Common Response Integrations</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Tool Type</th>
                <th>Example Action</th>
                <th>Automation Risk Level</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>EDR</strong></td>
                <td>Isolate a compromised host from the network.</td>
                <td>High, reversible but disruptive</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Network / Firewall</strong></td>
                <td>Block a malicious IP address.</td>
                <td>Medium</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Identity / Email</strong></td>
                <td>Disable an account or quarantine a mailbox.</td>
                <td>High, affects user access directly</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="warning-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> High-Risk Actions Need Approval Gates</h4>
        <p>Actions that disrupt a user or system, such as disabling an account or isolating a production host, should pause for analyst approval before executing. Fully automating high-impact actions without a checkpoint risks shutting down legitimate activity on a false positive.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

// TASK 6: Engineering Playbooks for SOC Workflows
$task_contents[6] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Putting It Together</h2>

    <p>Playbook architecture, trigger design, and integrations only matter once they are applied to a real workflow. Three of the most common playbooks built in a SOC are phishing triage, malware alert investigation, and account compromise response.</p>

    <h3>Three Common SOC Playbooks</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Workflow</th>
                <th>Trigger Type</th>
                <th>Key Stages</th>
                <th>Primary Response Action</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Phishing Triage</td>
                <td>Event-driven</td>
                <td>Extract IOCs, check threat intel, notify user</td>
                <td>Quarantine mailbox</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Malware Alert Investigation</td>
                <td>Alert-driven</td>
                <td>Sandbox detonation, ticket update</td>
                <td>Isolate host via EDR</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Account Compromise Response</td>
                <td>Alert-driven</td>
                <td>Session review, MFA enforcement</td>
                <td>Session termination and password reset</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Worked Example: Phishing Triage Playbook</h3>
    <p>A user reports a suspicious email, which creates a ticket tagged phishing-reported. That ticket firing is the event-driven trigger. The playbook extracts the sender address and any links as IOCs, checks them against the threat intelligence feed, and if the indicators are confirmed malicious, quarantines the mailbox and notifies the user. The ticket is then updated and closed automatically.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Design for Maintainability</h4>
        <p>It is tempting to build one large playbook with branching logic for every possible outcome. Smaller, focused playbooks that call each other are easier to test, debug, and update as tools and workflows change.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

